"id":109063683126539,"type":"genre","name":"Hard Rock","n":1},{"id":18872195504512,"type":"genre","name":"Pop Rock","n":1}],"labels":[],"album_labels":[],"own_scenes":[],"scenes":[{"id":114220518690419,"name":"Techno-rock y pop de estudio","book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","start":1973,"end":1989,"desc":"El «sonido» por encima de la melodía, el ritmo y la armonía: Electric Light Orchestra inventa el «high-tech pop»; Supertramp, Alan Parsons Project, Kansas, Styx y Split Enz. El valor de la producción define a Steely Dan (el pop-jazz pulido de los yuppies), Hall & Oates, los Fleetwood Mac de Rumours y Tusk, Toto, el «muro de sonido» titánico de Jim Steinman (Bat Out Of Hell) y la Penguin Cafe Orchestra. A finales de los setenta, el art-rock culmina en los supergrupos: 10cc, Godley & Creme, Foreigner, UK, Asia, Sky.","color":"#f472b6","family":"Pop, power-pop y synth-pop","link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#studio-pop"}],"book_tracks":[{"id":91090797954889,"artist":"Foreigner","artist_id":65748228157645,"title":"Double Vision","year":1978,"ess":1,"yt":"7B8pZvCWLUM","kind":"a","book":{"id":8,"title":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/"},"scene":"Techno-rock y pop de estudio","scene_id":114220518690419,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#studio-pop","chapters":["Sección 4.5"],"pages":[[1,95,null]]}],"mentions":[{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":55,"text":"…epic. Mediterranean and Slavic influences emerged more clearly in the forceful, noisy melodramas of Catch Bull At Four (1972) and in the suite Foreigner (1973). A few veterans of the early British Invasion managed to reinvent themselves in the age of progressive-rock. Rod Stewart (2) was the raucous,…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":95,"text":"…(1976), that used a new technique to produce symphonic, choral and natural sounds, the first in a series of pretentiously bizarre albums. Foreigner (1), formed by former Spooky Tooth's guitarist Mick Jones and by former King Crimson's keyboardist Ian McDonald, played a poppier, slickly-arranged…"}],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":"\"Hit-Making Machine\" Designation","body":"From the outset, Foreigner demonstrated an ability to consistently produce hit songs, solidifying their reputation as a \"hit-making machine.\" \"They proved right out of the gate that they were going to be a hit-making machine.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 58 - WKRP in Cincinnati","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 58 - WKRP in Cincinnati","url":"https://www.ftr70.com"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Audience Appreciation for Polish","body":"Despite critical assessments, the polished sound that critics dubbed \"Corporate Rock\" was precisely what Foreigner's audience sought and appreciated in their music. \"But to the band's audience, that polish was the whole point.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 58 - WKRP in Cincinnati","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 58 - WKRP in Cincinnati","url":"https://www.ftr70.com"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Critical Labeling as \"Corporate Rock\"","body":"Critics frequently referred to Foreigner as \"Corporate Rock,\" alleging that the band prioritized slick production and commercially viable choruses over artistic innovation and risk-taking. \"Reviewers often called Forerner Corporate Rock, they accused the band of making it a priority to have slick production and big choruses over taking any sort of artistic risk.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 58 - WKRP in Cincinnati","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 58 - WKRP in Cincinnati","url":"https://www.ftr70.com"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"End of Collaboration with Mut Lang","body":"After \"4\" and ACDC's \"For Those About to Rock,\" Mut Lang moved on from Foreigner, never producing another LP for the group again. \"After for those about to rock and for, Mutt Lang moved on from ACDC and Foriner.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"Pour Some Sugar on Me Edition Part 1","url":null},{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"Pour Some Sugar on Me Edition Part 1","url":"https://shows.acast.com/hit-parade/episodes/695d4ef5d1ba84fb8f04493d"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Ian McDonald's Death from Cancer","body":"Ian McDonald, also a member of Foreigner, died from cancer on February 9th at the age of 76. \"Ian McDolle was a member of King Crimson and Forner, Colin Cancer, February 9th. He was 76.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"In Memoriam of Those Lost in 2022","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sdPeii-8Ysw"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Pre-Mutt Success","body":"In the late 1970s, Foreigner had already achieved three platinum, top-five LPs filled with strutting arena rock jams, but had never reached number one on the album chart until Mut Lang's involvement. \"In the late 70s, Foriner had already racked up three platinum top five LP's packed with strutting Arena Rock Jams.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"Pour Some Sugar on Me Edition Part 1","url":null},{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"Pour Some Sugar on Me Edition Part 1","url":"https://shows.acast.com/hit-parade/episodes/695d4ef5d1ba84fb8f04493d"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Radio-Friendly and Arena-Ready Sound","body":"The band's musical style blended British rock polish with strong song hooks, making their music perfectly suited for both radio play and selling out large arenas across the United States. \"Forerner had this radio-friendly blend of British rock polish with these song hooks that were perfect for playing in sold out arenas in the US.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 58 - WKRP in Cincinnati","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 58 - WKRP in Cincinnati","url":"https://www.ftr70.com"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Rapid Rise in American Rock","body":"By the late 1970s, Foreigner had quickly become one of America's fastest-rising rock bands, known for immediately producing hit songs. \"By the late 1970s, Forerner had become one of the fastest rising rock bands in America, formed by songwriter Mick Jones, with powerhouse vocalist Lou Graham, the band arrived ready to make hits.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 58 - WKRP in Cincinnati","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 58 - WKRP in Cincinnati","url":"https://www.ftr70.com"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Rock and Roll Hall of Fame Recognition","body":"Foreigner is recognized as an inductee into the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame, a distinction mentioned in connection with their hit song \"Hot Blooded.\" \"Hot Blooded, that is the second single from Double Vision, from the Rock and Roll Hall of Famers Forerner.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 58 - WKRP in Cincinnati","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 58 - WKRP in Cincinnati","url":"https://www.ftr70.com"}]}],"awards":[{"kind":"award","title":"Grammy Award for Best New Artist (1977)","body":"Nominated.","year":1977,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1453643"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"Grammy Award for Best New Artist (1978)","body":"Nominated.","year":1978,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1453643"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"American Music Award for Favorite Pop/Rock Album (1982) — 4","body":"Nominated.","year":1982,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q19858080"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"Juno Award for International Album of the Year (1983) — 4","body":"Nominated.","year":1983,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q6314083"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"Rock and Roll Hall of Fame (2024)","body":"Won.","year":2024,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q179191"}]}],"concerts":[{"kind":"concert","title":"Farm Aid","body":null,"year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[]}],"xref":[{"kind":"xref","title":"Opening for Toto (January 26, 1979)","body":"In a misguided attempt by major record labels to market punk, The Ramones were chosen to open shows for Toto. This unusual pairing occurred on one tour, including a date in Lake Charles, Louisiana, on January 26, 1979, where they also shared the bill with Foreigner and Blue Oyster Cult. \"the Ramones were picked to open shows for Toto.\"","year":1979,"extra":{"from":"The Ramones","from_id":196070018852501,"type":"curiosity"},"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"The History of Alt-Rock: Chapter 8","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XjTWcy8RHEs"}]}],"charts":[{"id":159291170562362,"type":"song","name":"Feels Like the First Time","work_year":1977,"list_id":68626821091776,"list":"Billboard Year-End 1977","src":"singles","rank":31,"year":1977,"note":"","yt":"CK6jKL2qWxo","scaruffi":null},{"id":77032961180985,"type":"song","name":"Cold as Ice","work_year":1977,"list_id":68626821091776,"list":"Billboard Year-End 1977","src":"singles","rank":68,"year":1977,"note":"","yt":"ySb1f9zWJkQ","scaruffi":null},{"id":159291170562362,"type":"song","name":"Feels Like the First Time","work_year":1977,"list_id":13233582484549